  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <h2 class="section-title">Passive Activity Limits</h2>
      <div class="subsection-title">Closely held corporation.</div>
      <p>A closely held corporation can pass through income, losses, deductions, and credits to its shareholders. If the corporation engages in a passive activity, the shareholders are generally treated as if they engaged in the passive activity.</p>
      <p>If the corporation engages in a nonpassive activity, the shareholders are generally treated as if they engaged in the nonpassive activity.</p>
      <p>For more information, see Publication 563.</p>
      <div class="subsection-title">Rental Real Estate Professional</div>
      <p>Generally, rental activities are passive activities. However, if you meet certain requirements, you may be able to treat your rental activities as nonpassive. To qualify as a real estate professional, you must meet all of the following requirements:</p>
      <ul class="list-item">
        <li>You must materially participate in real property trades or businesses.</li>
        <li>You must devote substantially all of your time to real property trades or businesses.</li>
        <li>You must meet the requirements for a real estate professional under Revenue Code section 469(c)(7).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you meet these requirements, your rental activities may be treated as nonpassive.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h2 class="section-title">Passive Activity Deductions</h2>
      <p>Passive activity deductions include all deductions from passive activities that are not specifically excluded by the rules in this publication. These deductions are limited to the amount of passive income from the activity.</p>
      <p>Passive activity deductions do not include:</p>
      <ul class="list-item">
        <li>Deductions for expenses other than interest, taxes, and depreciation.</li>
        <li>Deductions for expenses that are not ordinary and necessary.</li>
        <li>Deductions for expenses that are not related to the activity.</li>
        <li>Deductions for expenses that are not allocable to the activity.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The deduction for one half of self-reimbursement is limited to the amount of passive income from the activity.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
      <div style="width: 50%;">
        <h3 class="subsection-title">Passive Activity Income</h3>
        <p>Passive activity income is income from passive activities. This income is generally not limited by the passive activity rules.</p>
        <p>Passive activity income includes:</p>
        <ul class="list-item">
          <li>Rental income.</li>
          <li>Income from royalties.</li>
          <li>Income from partnerships.</li>
          <li>Income from trusts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div style="width: 45%;">
        <h3 class="subsection-title">Recharacterization of Passive Income</h3>
        <p>Net income from the following passive activities may be recharacterized as nonpassive income:</p>
        <ul class="list-item">
          <li>Income from a trade or business in which you materially participate.</li>
          <li>Income from a qualified rental activity.</li>
          <li>Income from a real estate professional activity.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For more information, see Publication 563.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

