        <li>You must meet the requirements for a real estate professional under Revenue Code section 469(c)(7).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you meet these requirements, your rental activities may be treated as nonpassive.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 45%;">
      <h2 class="section-title">Disposition of Property Interests</h2>
      <p>Gain or loss from the disposition of an interest in a passive activity is generally passive gain or loss. However, if the activity is not passive, the gain or loss is not passive.</p>
      <p>If you dispose of an interest in a passive activity, the gain or loss is generally passive. However, if the activity is not passive, the gain or loss is not passive.</p>
      <p>For more information, see Publication 563.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between;">
      <div style="width: 50%;">
        <h3 class="subsection-title">Passive Activity Income</h3>
        <p>Passive activity income is income from passive activities. This income is generally not limited by the passive activity rules.</p>
        <p>Passive activity income includes:</p>
        <ul class="list-item">
          <li>Rental income.</li>
          <li>Income from royalties.</li>
          <li>Income from partnerships.</li>
          <li>Income from trusts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div style="width: 45%;">
        <h3 class="subsection-title">Exceptions for substantially appreciated property</h3>
        <p>If you dispose of property that has substantially appreciated, the gain or loss may be treated as nonpassive, even if the activity was passive.</p>
        <p>For more information, see Publication 563.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
